      "notes": [
        "This is a no-ball in-place overhand throw: no visible object, object load, collision, release event or flight is simulated.",
        "The CMU 124_01 Baseball Pitch trial is used to check the windup/cocking/acceleration/deceleration order. These pelvis-relative task points and this fixed-foot retarget are engineering-authored; they are not a direct motion-capture replay or measured individual biomechanics.",
        "The intended chain is a small bilateral knee/hip load, pelvis rotation before thorax rotation, then throwing-arm acceleration; after the notional release the torso flexes forward and the arm decelerates across the body. The feet remain fixed because this build is an in-place chain study, so it does not model a pitcher stride or measured ground-reaction impulses.",
        "The follow-through flexion targets were changed from an observed posterior arch to modest forward flexion, consistent with the deceleration phase in overhand pitching references. The chosen values are conservative engineering retargets, not athlete-specific measurements.",
        "The hand stays cupped through the wind-up and opens at the notional release. The 416-muscle forward record is authoritative for body and arm motion, and its physical wrist and finger states remain in the record. The reduced muscle response does not fully match the requested visible wrist/finger release pose, so the continuous skin hand uses the bounded authored wrist-flexion and finger-joint keyframes as a display retarget. This is an explicit engineering approximation, not a claim that the muscle simulation fully produced that hand release. MyoSim flexion_r is an anatomical wrist coordinate: negative is extension during cocking and positive is flexion during release. The 250°→80° request is treated as planar orientation, not literal wrist ROM; the physical wrist approaches the model’s ±45° soft limit, with the small measured residual reported in the record audit."
      ],
